"Geometry of poset antimatroids (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1571065313000322)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Geometry of poset antimatroids\n\n## Electronic Notes in Discrete Mathematics\n\nDate: 15 May 2013\n\nPages: 169-173\n\nVolume: Volume 40\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# Geometry of poset antimatroids\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Yulia Kempner, Vadim E. Levit\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.endm.2013.05.031\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nAn antimatroid is an accessible set system closed under union. A poset antimatroid is a particular case of antimatroid, which is formed by the lower sets of a poset. Feasible sets in a poset antimatroid ordered by inclusion form a distributive lattice, and every distributive lattice can be formed in this way. We introduce the polydimension of an antimatroid as the minimum dimension d such that the antimatroid may be isometrically embedded into d-dimensional integer lattice {\\mathbb{Z}}^{d}. We prove that every antimatroid of poly-dimension 2 is a poset antimatroid, and demonstrate both graph and geometric characterizations of such antimatroids. Finally, a conjecture concerning poset antimatroids of arbitrary poly-dimension d is presented.\n\n## Keywords\n\nAntimatroid\n\n;\n\nposet antimatroid\n\n;\n\ndimension\n\n\nView full text--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlso available at http://amc.imfm.si (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=37c0c292ea3f2de786d9653b65b36c47bc89f500&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn0search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Poly-antimatroid polyhedra ... Keywords: Antimatroid, polyhedron, convex dimension, lattice animal, polyomino.\nAlso available at http://amc.imfm.si\nISSN 1855-3966 (printed edn.), ISSN 1855-3974 (electronic edn.)\nARS MATHEMATICA CONTEMPORANEA 7 (2014) 73–82\nPoly-antimatroid polyhedra\nYulia Kempner\nHolon Institute of Technology, Israel\nVadim E. Levit\nAriel University, Israel\nReceived 31 October 2011, accepted 28 October 2012, published online 4 March 2013\nAbstract\nThe notion of “antimatroid with repetition” was conceived by Bjorner, Lovasz and Shor\nin 1991 as an extension of the notion of antimatroid in the framework of non-simple lan-\nguages. Further they were investigated by the name of “poly-antimatroids” (Nakamura,\n2005, Kempner & Levit, 2007), where the set system approach was used. If the underlying\nset of a poly-antimatroid consists of n elements, then the poly-antimatroid may be repre-\nsented as a subset of the integer lattice Zn. We concentrate on geometrical properties of\ntwo-dimensional (n = 2) poly-antimatroids - poly-antimatroid polygons, and prove that\nthese polygons are parallelogram polyominoes. We also show that each two-dimensional\npoly-antimatroid is a poset poly-antimatroid, i.e., it is closed under intersection.\nThe convex dimension cdim(S) of a poly-antimatroid S is the minimum number of\nmaximal chains needed to realize S. While the convex dimension of an n-dimensional\npoly-antimatroid may be arbitrarily large, we prove that the convex dimension of an n-\ndimensional poset poly-antimatroid is equal to n.\nKeywords: Antimatroid, polyhedron, convex dimension, lattice animal, polyomino.\nMath. Subj. Class.: 05B35\n1\nPreliminaries\nAn antimatroid is an accessible set system closed under union [3]. An algorithmic charac-\nterization of antimatroids based on the language deﬁnition was introduced in [5]. Another\nalgorithmic characterization of antimatroids that depicted them as set systems was devel-\noped in [14]. Dilworth (1940) was the ﬁrst to study antimatroids, using another axiomatiza-\ntion based on lattice theory, and they have been frequently rediscovered in other contexts.\nThe most updated survey on the subject may be found in [19].\nE-mail addresses: yuliak@hit.ac.il (Yulia Kempner), levitv@ariel.ac.il (Vadim E. Levit)\nCopyright c⃝2014 DMFA Slovenije--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPoly-antimatroid polyhedra (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/287337682_Poly-antimatroid_polyhedra)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; poly-antimatroid is a poset poly-antimatroid, i.e., it is closed under intersection.\nAuthor content\n\nAll content in this area was uploaded by Yulia Kempner on Jan 16, 2016\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\nAlso available at http://amc.imfm.si\n\nISSN 1855-3966 (printed edn.), ISSN 1855-3974 (electronic edn.)\n\nARS MATHEMATICA CONTEMPORANEA 7 (2014) 73–82\n\nPoly-antimatroid polyhedra\n\nYulia Kempner\n\nHolon Institute of Technology, Israel\n\nVadim E. Levit\n\nAriel University, Israel\n\nReceived 31 October 2011, accepted 28 October 2012, published online 4 March 2013\n\nAbstract\n\nThe notion of “antimatroid with repetition” was conceived by Bjorner, Lovasz and Shor\n\nin 1991 as an extension of the notion of antimatroid in the framework of non-simple lan-\n\nguages. Further they were investigated by the name of “poly-antimatroids” (Nakamura,\n\n2005, Kempner & Levit, 2007), where the set system approach was used. If the underlying\n\nset of a poly-antimatroid consists of n elements, then the poly-antimatroid may be repre-\n\nsented as a subset of the integer lattice Z n. We concentrate on geometrical properties of\n\ntwo-dimensional (n= 2) poly-antimatroids - poly-antimatroid polygons, and prove that\n\nthese polygons are parallelogram polyominoes. We also show that each two-dimensional\n\npoly-antimatroid is a poset poly-antimatroid, i.e., it is closed under intersection.\n\nThe convex dimension cdim(S)of a poly-antimatroid S is the minimum number of\n\nmaximal chains needed to realize S. While the convex dimension of an n-dimensional\n\npoly-antimatroid may be arbitrarily large, we prove that the convex dimension of an n-\n\ndimensional poset poly-antimatroid is equal to n.\n\nKeywords: Antimatroid, polyhedron, convex dimension, lattice animal, polyomino.\n\nMath. Subj. Class.: 05B35\n\n1 Preliminaries\n\nAn antimatroid is an accessible set system closed under union [3]. An algorithmic charac-\n\nterization of antimatroids based on the language deﬁnition was introduced in [5]. Another\n\nalgorithmic characterization of antimatroids that depicted them as set systems was devel-\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndLib.si - Poly-antimatroid polyhedra (https://www.dlib.si/details/URN%3ANBN%3ASI%3Adoc-5FQG3FNZ)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.4 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; # Poly-antimatroid polyhedra\n\n# Poly-antimatroid polyhedra\n\nImage: tab1\n\n1. PDF datoteka (273 kB)\n\n1. TXT datoteka (23 kB)\n\n   Izvoz v EndNote (RIS format)\n\nAvtor(ji)\n\nKempner, Yulia (avtor)\nLevit, Vadim E. (avtor)\n\nJezik\n\nangleški\n\nVir\n\nArs mathematica contemporanea\n\nLeto\n\n2014\n\nŠtevilčenje\n\nletnik 7, številka 1\n\nVsebina\n\nkombinatorika   matematika\n\nZaložnik\n\nDruštvo matematikov, fizikov in astronomov Slovenije\n\nIzvor\n\nUniverza na Primorskem, Fakulteta za naravoslovje, matematiko in informacijske tehnologije\n(Obvezni izvod spletne publikacije)\n\n\nURN\n\nURN:NBN:SI:doc-5FQG3FNZ\n\nCOBISSID\n\n16793177\n\nObjavljeno\n\n29.05.2020\n\nMetapodatki\n\nCitiranje\n\nAPA:\n\nKempner, Yulia, Levit, Vadim E. (2014). Poly-antimatroid polyhedra. Ars mathematica contemporanea, letnik 7, številka 1, str. 73-82. URN:NBN:SI:doc-5FQG3FNZ from http://www.dlib.si\n\nMLA:\n\nKempner, Yulia, Levit, Vadim E.. \"Poly-antimatroid polyhedra.\" Ars mathematica contemporanea letnik 7. številka 1 (2014) str. 73-82.\n<http://www.dlib.si/?URN=URN:NBN:SI:doc-5FQG3FNZ>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Geometric Characterization of Poly-antimatroids (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1571065307000522)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago;   * ### Poly-antimatroid polyhedra\n\n## Electronic Notes in Discrete Mathematics\n\nVolume 28, 1 March 2007, Pages 357-364\n\n# A Geometric Characterization of Poly-antimatroids\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.endm.2007.01.050Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe concept of \"antimatroid with repetition\" was coined by Bjorner, Lovasz and Shor in 1991 as an extension of the notion of antimatroid in the framework of non-simple languages [Björner A., L. Lovász, and P. R. Shor, Chip-firing games on graphs, European Journal of Combinatorics 12 (1991), 283–291]. There are some equivalent ways to define antimatroids. They may be separated into two categories: antimatroids defined as set systems and antimatroids defined as languages. For poly-antimatroids we use the set system approach. In this research we concentrate on interrelations between geometric, algorithmic, and lattice properties of poly-antimatroids. Much to our surprise it turned out that even the two-dimensional case is not trivial.\n\n## Access through your organization\n\nCheck access to the full text by signing in through your organization.\n\nAccess through your organization\n\n## References (8)\n\n\n## Cited by (2)\n\n  * ### Distributive Lattice Polyhedra\n\n2009, Electronic Notes in Discrete Mathematics\n\nShow abstract\n\nPoly-antimatroids are generalization of the notion of antimatroid to multisets. When the underlying set consists of only two elements, such two-dimensional poly-antimatroids correspond to point sets in the integer lattice {\\mathbb{Z}}^{d}${\\mathbb{Z}}^{d}$. In this research we concentrate on geometrical properties of two-dimensional poly-antimatroids and prove that these sets form distributive lattice polyhedra. Our findings imply that two-dimensional poly-antimatroids have convex dimension 2. Further we investigate geometrical properties of three-dimensional distributive lattice polyhedra.\n\n  * ### Poly-antimatroid polyhedra\n\n2014, Ars Mathematica Contemporanea\n\nView full text--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGeometry of poset antimatroids (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/257642230_Geometry_of_poset_antimatroids)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; # Geometry of poset antimatroids ... We prove that every antimatroid of poly-dimension 2 is a poset antimatroid, and demonstrate both graph and geometric characterizations of such antimatroids.\n\nArticle\n\n# Geometry of poset antimatroids\n\n  * May 2013\n  * Electronic Notes in Discrete Mathematics 40:169–173\n\nDOI:10.1016/j.endm.2013.05.031\n\nAuthors:\n\nYulia Kempner\n\n  * Holon Institute of Technology\n\nVadim Levit\n\n  * Ariel University\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research, you can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\n## Abstract\n\nAn antimatroid is an accessible set system closed under union. A poset antimatroid is a particular case of antimatroid, which is formed by the lower sets of a poset. Feasible sets in a poset antimatroid ordered by inclusion form a distributive lattice, and every distributive lattice can be formed in this way. We introduce the polydimension of an antimatroid as the minimum dimension d such that the antimatroid may be isometrically embedded into d-dimensional integer lattice ZdZd. We prove that every antimatroid of poly-dimension 2 is a poset antimatroid, and demonstrate both graph and geometric characterizations of such antimatroids. Finally, a conjecture concerning poset antimatroids of arbitrary poly-dimension d is presented.\n\n## No full-text available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\n## Citations (4)\n\n## References (7)\n\n... Nakamura obtains a characterization of poset antimatroids by singleelement extensions in [21] and by excluded minors in [22]. Recently, Kempner and Levit [17] introduce the poly-dimension of an antimatroid, prove that every antimatroid of poly-dimension 2 is a poset antimatroid, and establish both graph and geometric characterizations of such antimatroids. From the optimization point of view, poset antimatroids with weighted elements are easy to study. ...\n\n... A circuit is a minimal nonfree subset of V . An equivalent characterization reads (for a proof, see for instance [17]): a subset C of V is a circuit if and only if Tr(F , C) = 2 C \\ {{r}} for some r in C (this element r is unique) . The element r is then the root of C, and the pair (C \\ {r}, r) is a rooted circuit. ...\n\nOn the shelling antimatroids of split graphs\n\nArticle\n\n  * Dec 2015\n  * Electron Notes Discrete Math\n\n  * Jean Cardinal\n  * Jean-Paul Doignon\n  * Keno Merckx\n\nUnlike poset antimatroids, chordal graph shelling antimatroids have received little attention as regard their structures, optimization properties and associated circuits. Here we consider a special case of those antimatroids, namely the split graph shelling antimatroids. We establish a connection between the structure of split graph shelling antimatroids and poset shelling antimatroids. We discuss some applications of this new connection, in particular, we give a simple polynomial time algorithm to find a maximum (or minimum) weight feasible set in split graph shelling antimatroids and list all the circuits and free sets for this class of antimatroids.\n\nPoly-Dimension of Antimatroids\n\nChapter\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Mar 2012\n\n  * Yulia Kempner\n  * Vadim Levit\n\nView\n\nA function F defined on the family of all subsets of a finite ground set E is quasi-concave, if F(X∪Y)≥min{F(X),F(Y)} for all X,Y⊆E. Quasi-concave functions arise in many fields of mathematics and computer science such as social choice, graph theory, data mining, clustering and other fields. The maximization of a quasi-concave function takes, in general, exponential time. However, if a quasi-concave function is defined by an associated monotone linkage function, then it can be optimized by a greedy type algorithm in polynomial time. Recently, quasi-concave functions defined as minimum values of monotone linkage functions were considered on antimatroids, where the correspondence between quasi-concave and bottleneck functions was shown Kempner and Levit (2003) [6]. The goal of this paper is to analyze quasi-concave functions on different families of sets and to investigate their relationships with monotone linkage functions.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nCharacterizations of polygreedoids and poly-antimatroids by greedy algorithms\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jul 2005--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDigitalna knjižnica Slovenije - dLib.si (https://dlib.si/results/?pageSize=25&query=%27contributor%3DKempner%2C+Yulia%27)\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Poly-antimatroid polyhedra\n\n  * [Input] Digitalna knjižnica Slovenije\n  * [Input] Narodna in univerzitetna knjižnica\n  * Splošne knjižnice\n    * Celjsko območje\n      * [Input] Vse knjižnice območja\n\nŠtevilo rezultatov iskanja: 1\n\n* * *\n\nTip gradiva\n\nznanstveno časopisje in članki (1)\n\nČasopisje in članki - naslov\n\nArs mathematica contemporanea (1)\n\nČasopisje in članki - oblika\n\nčlanki (1)\n\nJezik\n\nangleški (1)\n\nLeto izida\n\n2014 (1)\n\nUniverza na Primorskem, Fakulteta za naravoslovje, matematiko in informacijske tehnologije (1)\n\nVsebina\n\nMatematika (1)\n\nZaložnik\n\nDruštvo matematikov, fizikov in astronomov Slovenije (1)\n\nPravice\n\nCC BY - priznanje avtorstva (1)\n\nDostop\n\nProst (1)\n\nRazvrsti po:\n\n  * ustreznosti\n\n    * ustreznosti\n    * datumu naraščajoče\n    * datumu padajoče\n    * datumu objave naraščajoče\n    * datumu objave padajoče\n    * naslovu A-Z\n    * naslovu Z-A\n\nPoly-antimatroi